{# ── Short answer ── #}
<section class="section">
  <div class="container container--narrow">
    <div class="callout">
      <p><strong>The short answer:</strong> yes, many visa holders can buy a home in Australia. What you can buy, and how much a lender will lend, comes down to your visa. Permanent residents living here are treated much like citizens. Temporary residents need FIRB approval, can't buy an established home until 30 June 2029, and in NSW usually pay a 9% surcharge on top of normal stamp duty. Partner visa holders buying with a citizen or PR spouse often have the easiest path.</p>
    </div>
    <p>Everyone's situation is different, so this is general information, not advice.</p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Foreign person ── #}
<section class="section section--cool">
  <div class="container container--narrow">
    <h2>Are you a "foreign person" for property rules?</h2>
    <p>For federal property rules you're generally a foreign person if you're not an Australian citizen, not a permanent resident who's ordinarily resident here, and not a New Zealand citizen on a special category visa (subclass 444). Temporary visa holders, including most bridging visa holders, are foreign persons.</p>
    <p>State tax rules use their own tests. In NSW, holding PR isn't enough on its own. More on that below.</p>
  </div>
</section>

